    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n63862" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">Λοξ-ίας</span></b> , <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ου</span>, 
			Ion.
		
		<b><span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">Λοξ-ίης</span></b> , <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">εω</span>, ὁ, epith. of Apollo, <i><span class="en">
			B.
			12.148
		</span></i>, <i><span class="en">
			Hdt.
			1.91
		</span></i>, <i><span class="en">
			A.
			<u>Eu.</u>
			19
		</span></i>,al., <i><span class="en">
			S.
			<u>OT</u>
			410
		</span></i>, <i><span class="en">
			Ar.
			<u>Eq.</u>
			1047
		</span></i>, <i><span class="en">
			<u>Pl.</u>
			8
		</span></i>, etc.; expld. because the Sun traverses the ecliptic (cf. II),
			<i><span class="en">
			Cleanth.Stoic.
			1.123
		</span></i>; or from Apollo's 'crooked', i.e. ambiguous, oracles (cf. <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>A.</b>
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">λοξός</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">
					3
				</span></i>
			), <i><span class="en">
				Corn.
				<u>ND</u>
				32
			</span></i>, etc. </div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense2"><b>II.</b> (<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">λοξός</span>) <i>the zodiac</i> or
			<i>ecliptic</i>, from its <i>obliquity</i> to the equator, <i><span class="en">
				Anon.
				<u>Intr.Arat.</u>
				p.96
			</span></i> M.</div>
